# ChatConnect — Frontend environment variables
# All Vite vars must start with VITE_.  These are PUBLIC (baked into the JS
# bundle at build time) — never put secrets here. Real secrets live only in the
# backend (server/.env locally, or the Render dashboard in production).
#
# ⚠ WHICH FILE WINS. Vite loads `.env` first, then `.env.<mode>` on top, and a
# production build runs in mode=production. So `client/.env.production` (which
# IS committed) overrides your local `client/.env` (which is NOT). The trap:
# anything you set ONLY in your local `.env` works in your own build and is
# silently MISSING from a CI/Vercel build, because that machine has no `.env`.
# TURN is the one that bites — see the WebRTC section below.

# ── Local development ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave VITE_API_URL blank to use the Vite dev proxy (recommended locally),
# and set VITE_DEMO_MODE=false to talk to the real backend + database.
VITE_API_URL=
VITE_SOCKET_URL=
VITE_DEMO_MODE=false

# ── Production (set these in the Vercel dashboard, NOT in a committed file) ──
# VITE_API_URL   = https://your-backend.onrender.com        (with or without /api — both work)
# VITE_SOCKET_URL= https://your-backend.onrender.com        (backend origin, NO /api)
# VITE_DEMO_MODE = false
# Then on the backend (Render) set CLIENT_URL = https://your-frontend.vercel.app
# so CORS allows your deployed frontend origin.

# ── WebRTC calling ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Google STUN works on same-LAN / localhost. For calls across the internet /
# mobile networks you MUST add a TURN server (Twilio, Metered.ca, or self-hosted
# coturn) — STUN alone can't punch through strict NATs; without TURN those calls
# ring but the media never connects and the call auto-drops.
#
# ⚠ These MUST be set wherever the PRODUCTION bundle is built (the Vercel
# project's env vars, or `client/.env.production`). Setting them only in your
# local `client/.env` makes calls work on your machine and fail for everyone
# else — see the precedence note at the top of this file.
#
# Prefer VITE_TURN_CREDENTIALS_URL over the static trio: static TURN credentials
# are readable by anyone who opens the JS bundle, and TURN relays are metered,
# so a leaked pair is someone else's bandwidth on your bill.
# Static credentials:
VITE_TURN_URL=
VITE_TURN_USERNAME=
VITE_TURN_CREDENTIAL=
# OR a time-limited credentials endpoint (e.g. metered.ca:
# https://<your-app>.metered.live/api/v1/turn/credentials?apiKey=<key>).
# Response may be one ice-server object or an array of them.
VITE_TURN_CREDENTIALS_URL=

